      "title": "Creating Parallel Vectors"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "f38f9344-7813-585f-9469-6322fec9ad2c",
      ":question/problem": "![](/tmp/course-academy-g2-readonly-tly0pcma/17592186096649/caller-moderate/generated/f84429a171f26681785337b22cccc67e26abd84d48ee54166d9cfb0c583d6caf.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $-2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}$?",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that both $-2\\mathbf{a}$ and $-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ must be parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nNow, for the vector $-2\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-2\\mathbf{a}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-2\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions since the factor $-2$ is negative.\n\n![](/tmp/course-academy-g2-readonly-tly0pcma/17592186096649/caller-moderate/generated/f63b1c1a6b8d7929015e65109b3f9e11375332f08bda78d10433b941bfb9d84e.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ is $\\frac{3}{2}$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-\\frac{3}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{3}{2}$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](/tmp/course-academy-g2-readonly-tly0pcma/17592186096649/caller-moderate/generated/8c4c20717972f28bd23b09e7057adc7300d329ccee17c92b9c50f50da95268e5.png)",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
