    "question": {
      ":question/id": "fd1e6414-2bfc-59fc-b704-1767cb39f38a",
      ":question/problem": "![](/tmp/course-academy-g2-readonly-tly0pcma/17592186096649/caller-moderate/generated/67b2906b8d863789d7447fd877afa47385e69c5ba8ce5d33a28d4ef8cf7e5c27.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $-\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{a}$ and $3\\mathbf{a}$?",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that both $-\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{a}$ and $3\\mathbf{a}$ must be parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nNow, for the vector $-\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{a}$ is $\\frac{3}{4}$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-\\frac{3}{4}\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions since the factor $-\\frac{3}{4}$ is negative.\n\n![](/tmp/course-academy-g2-readonly-tly0pcma/17592186096649/caller-moderate/generated/1ea5e6a7d9f7503cd77b1b079a395e2451538150165d614bebfc0c5de5b0e3b2.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $3\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $3\\mathbf{a}$ is $3$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $3\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction since the factor $3$ is positive.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](/tmp/course-academy-g2-readonly-tly0pcma/17592186096649/caller-moderate/generated/5d2625d760aa016bae988896bbcd89536bfaffe49bdb854cf7393048e17daa50.png)",
