    "question": {
      ":question/id": "04a3a50e-b16d-5560-bdde-37b13e134922",
      ":question/problem": "![](/tmp/course-academy-g2-readonly-tly0pcma/17592186096649/caller-moderate/generated/827970060fc6de1db6b3344a343e4ea76fecbc5764c7e6ed190bf7238ae53969.png)\n\nThe vector $\\mathbf{a}$ is depicted above. Which of the following diagrams shows the vectors $\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ and $-2\\mathbf{a}$?",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/moderate",
      ":question/worked-solution": "First, notice that both $\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ and $-2\\mathbf{a}$ must be parallel to $\\mathbf{a}$.\n\nNow, for the vector $\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ is $\\frac{1}{2}$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $\\frac{1}{2}\\mathbf{a}$ have the same direction since the factor $\\frac{1}{2}$ is positive.\n\n![](/tmp/course-academy-g2-readonly-tly0pcma/17592186096649/caller-moderate/generated/0fb027e81a811e5143cf5bae32327095e8c353821fa67d7461aaf513d454289f.png)\n\nNext, for the vector $-2\\mathbf{a}$ we have the following:\n\n- The magnitude of $-2\\mathbf{a}$ is $2$ times the magnitude of $\\mathbf{a}$.\n- Vectors $\\mathbf{a}$ and $-2\\mathbf{a}$ have opposite directions since the factor $-2$ is negative.\n\nThe required vectors are shown below.\n\n![](/tmp/course-academy-g2-readonly-tly0pcma/17592186096649/caller-moderate/generated/54cae49c28be248f1364e7c56844196b40b4fbf054679400204e77101cc7b55b.png)",
